<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ashvins</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Nasatyas, Ashvini Kumaras</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ashvins, also called the Nasatyas, are twin horsemen gods of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rigveda">Rigveda</a>, associated with dawn, healing and rescue. They are invoked as the Nasatyas, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitra">Mitra</a>, Varuna and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indra">Indra</a>, in a treaty of the 14th century BCE between the Hittite king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suppiluliuma-i">Suppiluliuma I</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shattiwaza">Shattiwaza</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitanni">Mitanni</a>, among the earliest evidence of Indo-Aryan religion. They have parallels in the Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castor-and-pollux">Dioscuri</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ashvins.jpg" alt="Ashvins" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: RIG VEDA. Dhumavati. los diez mahavydas de la Mitologia Hindu · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ashwini_Kumaras-L.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name Ashvin, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> for horse possessors, comes from an Indo-Iranian stem for the horse. The Ashvins are Nasatya and Dasra, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rigveda">Rigveda</a> they are always referred to in the dual, though the texts distinguish the two. They are often called the grandsons of Dyaus, the sky-god, a formula that matches the Lithuanian Ašvieniai, the Latvian Dieva Dēli and the Greek Diós-kouroi, the boys of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a>, who are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castor-and-pollux">Castor and Pollux</a>. They are one instance of the Indo-European divine horse twins, which may also lie behind the English Hengist and Horsa and the Welsh Bran and Manawydan.</p><p>The name Nasatya appears 99 times in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rigveda">Rigveda</a>, and is probably related to a root for safe return home. The first mention of the Nasatya twins is in a treaty of about 1350 BCE between Suppiluliuma, king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shattiwaza">Shattiwaza</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitanni">Mitanni</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rigveda">Rigveda</a> mentions the Ashvins 398 times, with more than 50 hymns addressed to them. They are youthful twin horsemen who travel in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a> drawn by tireless horses and who safeguard and rescue people. The chariot has three wheels and three chariot-boxes, and is drawn by horses, bulls, buffaloes and birds. They are linked with medicine, healing and the twilight, and they follow the dawn goddess Ushas, who is asked to awaken them. The Rigveda calls them slayers of darkness.</p><p>Their deeds of rescue are many. They drew the seer Rebha back from the waters after he had been bound and cast in for nine days and ten nights, and brought Bhujyu home from the sea. They freed Atri from a cleft in the earth, restored the eyesight of Rijrashva and gave Vishpala a metal foot. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shatapatha-brahmana">Shatapatha Brahmana</a> they restore the youth of the old sage Chyavana, and the other gods at first refused them a share in the sacrifice because they spent too much time among mortals.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>In ritual, honey is associated with the Ashvins, and they are the chief deities of the Pravargya rite, in which they receive hot milk. They are invoked at dawn, the time of their principal sacrifice. As a dual pair, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indra">Indra</a> and Vayu and with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitra">Mitra</a> and Varuna, they share in the morning pressing of soma, and they are the last to receive soma in the overnight Atirātra rite.</p><p>Later texts keep them important. Sources vary, but they are generally said to be sons of the sun god and his wife Sanjna, who is said to have taken the form of a mare. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a> tells that Madri invoked the Ashvins and bore the twins Nakula and Sahadeva. A local tradition holds that the Ashvins first prepared the formulation behind chyavanprash for the sage Chyavana.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ashvins” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A Mitanni treaty with the Hittites calls on the gods Mitra, Varuna, Indra and the <b>Nasatyas</b>, some of the earliest evidence for Indo-Aryan languages anywhere.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mitanni-empire">Mitanni Empire · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
